          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Pet publishing splits into four tiers, and where you can land a byline depends on the credentials behind it, not the size of your list.</p>

            <p>The major pet-media brands are vet-reviewed and mostly staff-written. The dog and cat verticals take contributions with the right expertise. The vet and insurance resources want licensed authors. The enthusiast blogs are wide open. The list of 100 is below, grouped roughly by tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Major pet-media brands</td><td>Chewy, PetMD, The Spruce Pets, Rover, AKC, Petfinder</td><td>Mostly not guest posts. A contribution from a licensed vet, certified trainer or named credentialed expert</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Dog &amp; cat verticals</td><td>Dogster, Catster, iHeartDogs, K9 of Mine, The Labrador Site, PetGuide</td><td>Freelance and contributor pitches; how-tos, breed guides, product testing with hands-on detail</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Vet &amp; pet-insurance resources</td><td>Preventive Vet, Whole Dog Journal, ASPCA Pet Insurance, Embrace, Figo</td><td>Contributed pieces from vets, vet nurses and behaviourists; strict on health claims</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Enthusiast &amp; niche blogs</td><td>ThatMutt, Puppy Leaks, All Things Dogs, The Discerning Cat, PetPress</td><td>Open to contributions; first-hand experience, breed and product angles, personal stories</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Spam Score</td><td>Moz's 0 to 4 estimate of how many spam signals a site shows</td><td>In this niche, treat 3 to 4 as a reason to look closely before you pitch</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> the pet niche is full of thin, ad-heavy blogs that all republish the same "15 human foods dogs can eat" article. A placement on a vet-reviewed brand or a vertical with real readers is worth more than a dozen of those, and it is the kind of link that survives an update.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 pet sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating, estimated monthly traffic and Spam Score. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; shut-down, parked and merged sites were swapped for live equivalents. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th><th>Spam</th></tr>
                </thead>
