                      <td>{s.n}</td>
                      <td>{s.site}</td>
                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                      <td>{s.spam}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for pet editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the credential or the hands-on.</strong> "As a small-animal vet" or "I tested 9 no-pull harnesses on two large dogs over 6 weeks" earns the read.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the angle the staff missed.</strong> Not "how to crate-train a puppy." Something like "why crate training fails for rescue dogs, and what to do instead."</li>
              <li><strong>Bring your own assets.</strong> Photos of your animals, test results, before-and-after. Pet editors will not run stock imagery for a hands-on piece.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the tier's standard.</strong> Health pieces need a vet author or reviewer. Training pieces need a certification. Lifestyle pieces need real experience.</li>
              <li><strong>Send one clip</strong> in a similar style and your short bio.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any brand relationship</strong> up front, especially for food, supplements and insurance.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if you had no product to promote? If yes, pitch it. If it is a walkthrough of your product's features, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a generic "best dog foods 2026" listicle. Every site has a rolling one already, and health claims need a vet.</li>
              <li>Send a health or nutrition piece with no veterinary author or reviewer. Serious pet sites will reject it.</li>
              <li>Republish the same "foods dogs can and cannot eat" article that is already on 200 other blogs.</li>
              <li>Stuff affiliate links, or point exact-match anchors ("best flea treatment") at your store.</li>
              <li>Use AI-generated filler with no first-hand detail. In pet content it reads as generic within a paragraph.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a few placements on vet-reviewed brands and verticals with real readers will do more for your rankings and sales than a long list of thin pet-blog links. Use the Spam Score and traffic columns to find your few.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>
